{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"like terms like terms","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md","fileName":"Like Terms.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Like Terms","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Algebraic Expressions]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algebraic Expressions","Numerical Coefficient","Combining Like Terms","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"like terms like terms","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md","fileName":"Like Terms.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Like Terms","heading":"Like Terms","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Like terms have the same variable factors raised to the same corresponding powers. Their order can differ because multiplication is commutative, so (4a^2b^3) and (7b^3a^2) are like terms.\n\nOnly their [[Numerical Coefficient]]s may differ. Terms such as (4a) and (4a^2), or (4ab^2) and (4ab^3), are unlike because at least one exponent differs. Matching structure permits [[Combining Like Terms]]; superficial similarity in letters is not enough.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Algebraic Expressions","Numerical Coefficient","Combining Like Terms","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"like terms like terms","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Like Terms.md","fileName":"Like Terms.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Like Terms","heading":"References","sectionNumber":